WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:03.070
- When scholars look back
on the 2016 election,

00:00:03.070 --> 00:00:06.050
one of the strangest,
most befuddling things,

00:00:06.050 --> 00:00:09.860
though by no means the strangest
or most befuddling things,

00:00:09.860 --> 00:00:12.950
will be that this guy was
the putative front runner

00:00:12.950 --> 00:00:14.500
for the democratic nominee

00:00:14.500 --> 00:00:16.726
for the president of the United States.

00:00:16.726 --> 00:00:18.950
And that's when things got weird.

00:00:18.950 --> 00:00:22.360
(victorious music)

00:00:22.360 --> 00:00:24.330
Michael Avenatti, the
attorney who rose to fame

00:00:24.330 --> 00:00:26.040
for his representation of Stormy Daniels

00:00:26.040 --> 00:00:27.490
in lawsuits against President Trump

00:00:27.490 --> 00:00:29.300
has now been convicted and sentenced

00:00:29.300 --> 00:00:30.970
to two and a half years in prison

00:00:30.970 --> 00:00:32.860
for attempting to extort Nike.

00:00:32.860 --> 00:00:35.690
And his legal journey is just beginning.

00:00:35.690 --> 00:00:37.710
By all accounts, it looks like
he's going to go to prison

00:00:37.710 --> 00:00:40.290
for a lot longer before
all of this is done.

00:00:40.290 --> 00:00:41.370
In Avenatti's journey

00:00:41.370 --> 00:00:43.010
from litigator for the resistance

00:00:43.010 --> 00:00:44.950
to disgraced heel of the legal profession

00:00:44.950 --> 00:00:48.050
is one of the steepest falls
that many can remember.

00:00:48.050 --> 00:00:50.320
And his legal troubles are far from over.

00:00:50.320 --> 00:00:52.130
In December, he will stand trial

00:00:52.130 --> 00:00:55.000
for allegedly stealing
$300,000 from Daniels.

00:00:55.000 --> 00:00:57.330
Right now, he is currently
on trial in Los Angeles

00:00:57.330 --> 00:01:01.420
on charges that he embezzled
$10 million from five clients.

00:01:01.420 --> 00:01:02.400
During jury selection,

00:01:02.400 --> 00:01:03.700
Avenatti fired his lawyer

00:01:03.700 --> 00:01:05.810
and got the green light
to represent himself.

00:01:05.810 --> 00:01:08.040
You might think that this
is a pretty standard move,

00:01:08.040 --> 00:01:09.150
but believe me,

00:01:09.150 --> 00:01:11.380
defendants representing
themselves in a criminal trial

00:01:11.380 --> 00:01:13.490
is the stuff of bad movies,

00:01:13.490 --> 00:01:16.080
like this one starring
Vin Diesel with hair.

00:01:16.080 --> 00:01:17.980
- Have you had any legal experience?

00:01:17.980 --> 00:01:20.270
- I've been in prison half my life.

00:01:20.270 --> 00:01:23.120
Sometimes I think I had
too much legal experience.

00:01:23.120 --> 00:01:26.680
- But you guys, this scene
actually happened in real life.

00:01:26.680 --> 00:01:27.740
Avenatti told the judge

00:01:27.740 --> 00:01:29.288
he was qualified to represent himself

00:01:29.288 --> 00:01:32.820
because he had just participated
in his own criminal trial.

00:01:32.820 --> 00:01:33.817
He told the judge,

00:01:33.817 --> 00:01:35.760
"I've made appearances in less than five,

00:01:35.760 --> 00:01:37.830
maybe two or three
criminal cases, Your Honor.

00:01:37.830 --> 00:01:39.550
I have never tried a criminal case."

00:01:39.550 --> 00:01:42.090
And then he also mentioned
his participation

00:01:42.090 --> 00:01:43.740
in the Nike extortion case

00:01:43.740 --> 00:01:45.990
in the Southern district of New York.

00:01:45.990 --> 00:01:48.150
When the judge asked about
Avenatti's participation

00:01:48.150 --> 00:01:49.150
in criminal sentencing,

00:01:49.150 --> 00:01:51.090
he again brought up the
time a few weeks ago

00:01:51.090 --> 00:01:53.580
when he was sentenced to two
and a half years in prison.

00:01:53.580 --> 00:01:55.950
This is literally a joke
from "The Simpsons".

00:01:55.950 --> 00:01:57.700
- And as for your case, don't you worry.

00:01:57.700 --> 00:02:00.060
I've argued in front of
every judge in the state,

00:02:00.060 --> 00:02:01.903
often as a lawyer.

00:02:01.903 --> 00:02:03.220
(sipping soda)

00:02:03.220 --> 00:02:04.505
- Of course,

00:02:04.505 --> 00:02:06.030
legal warnings about the folly
of representing yourself,

00:02:06.030 --> 00:02:08.160
date back all the way to 1682.

00:02:08.160 --> 00:02:09.137
There's a reason for the saying,

00:02:09.137 --> 00:02:11.670
"A man who represents himself
has a fool for a client."

00:02:11.670 --> 00:02:13.630
And today we're going
to answer the question

00:02:13.630 --> 00:02:16.640
just how foolish is Michael Avenatti?

00:02:16.640 --> 00:02:18.790
The answer, very.

00:02:18.790 --> 00:02:20.750
- It appears to be amateur hour.

00:02:20.750 --> 00:02:22.720
- So who is this guy, anyway?

00:02:22.720 --> 00:02:24.710
Any discussion of the Avenatti saga

00:02:24.710 --> 00:02:26.280
has to start with that question.

00:02:26.280 --> 00:02:28.470
And Michael Avenatti was not well-known

00:02:28.470 --> 00:02:30.160
outside of California legal circles

00:02:30.160 --> 00:02:31.410
until he burst onto the scene

00:02:31.410 --> 00:02:34.700
by suing Donald Trump on
behalf of Stormy Daniels.

00:02:34.700 --> 00:02:36.760
Avenatti's willingness to opine on Trump

00:02:36.760 --> 00:02:38.990
made him an attractive
guest for cable news hits.

00:02:38.990 --> 00:02:42.120
In fact, he made over 250
television news appearances

00:02:42.120 --> 00:02:43.680
in less than two years.

00:02:43.680 --> 00:02:45.720
And then he represented Julie Swetnick

00:02:45.720 --> 00:02:46.810
in a misconduct claim

00:02:46.810 --> 00:02:48.890
against Supreme Court
nominee Brett Kavanaugh,

00:02:48.890 --> 00:02:51.080
once again putting
himself front and center.

00:02:51.080 --> 00:02:53.150
But not everyone was an Avenatti fan.

00:02:53.150 --> 00:02:55.700
He got into an argument
with Michael Cohen's lawyer,

00:02:55.700 --> 00:02:58.440
whom he derided for representing a felon.

00:02:58.440 --> 00:03:01.370
This is astoundingly bad
commentary by Avenatti.

00:03:01.370 --> 00:03:02.470
Not to point out the obvious,

00:03:02.470 --> 00:03:05.900
but now he is such a felon
in need of representation

00:03:05.900 --> 00:03:07.620
though perhaps his philosophy

00:03:07.620 --> 00:03:10.030
is why he wants to represent himself.

00:03:10.030 --> 00:03:11.920
And Avenatti got into frequent arguments

00:03:11.920 --> 00:03:13.000
with just about everyone,

00:03:13.000 --> 00:03:15.300
including well-known former
prosecutor, Ken White,

00:03:15.300 --> 00:03:17.130
who you might know as Popehat on Twitter.

00:03:17.130 --> 00:03:18.160
And after one of their Twitter rows,

00:03:18.160 --> 00:03:20.210
Avenatti called up White to yell and curse

00:03:20.210 --> 00:03:21.043
and proclaim himself

00:03:21.043 --> 00:03:22.680
"The greatest lawyer who ever lawyered."

00:03:22.680 --> 00:03:24.980
which of course is incredibly ironic

00:03:24.980 --> 00:03:27.630
given his current location.

00:03:27.630 --> 00:03:29.200
- That is not a very smart guy.

00:03:29.200 --> 00:03:30.033
- Though many people

00:03:30.033 --> 00:03:31.480
thought he was a charlatan from the start,

00:03:31.480 --> 00:03:32.990
millions of people were fooled.

00:03:32.990 --> 00:03:34.320
His pugnacious TV appearances

00:03:34.320 --> 00:03:36.240
made him a popular Twitter personality

00:03:36.240 --> 00:03:38.150
and earned him speaking
engagements all over the country.

00:03:38.150 --> 00:03:39.530
At one point in 2017,

00:03:39.530 --> 00:03:41.920
Avenatti announced he was
considering running for president.

00:03:41.920 --> 00:03:43.310
He even visited Iowa

00:03:43.310 --> 00:03:45.230
to prepare for the presidential primary.

00:03:45.230 --> 00:03:46.063
Michael Avenatti

00:03:46.063 --> 00:03:47.790
looked like a man who
was king of the world,

00:03:47.790 --> 00:03:49.780
flying around the world on private jets

00:03:49.780 --> 00:03:51.660
and owning his own race car company.

00:03:51.660 --> 00:03:52.580
But behind the scenes,

00:03:52.580 --> 00:03:54.710
Avenatti was already cracking.

00:03:54.710 --> 00:03:55.940
Let's start at the beginning

00:03:55.940 --> 00:03:57.780
of Michael Avenatti's legal career.

00:03:57.780 --> 00:03:58.613
In the year 2000,

00:03:58.613 --> 00:04:00.570
Michael Avenatti graduated
first in his class

00:04:00.570 --> 00:04:02.820
from the George Washington
University School of Law.

00:04:02.820 --> 00:04:03.730
Just three years later,

00:04:03.730 --> 00:04:05.950
GW established the
Michael J. Avenatti Award

00:04:05.950 --> 00:04:08.400
for Excellence in Pre-Trial
and Trial Advocacy.

00:04:08.400 --> 00:04:10.280
Getting an award named
after you so quickly

00:04:10.280 --> 00:04:11.340
means two things.

00:04:11.340 --> 00:04:12.630
One, you are making a lot of money,

00:04:12.630 --> 00:04:15.370
and two, you are donating
a lot of that money,

00:04:15.370 --> 00:04:16.900
though Avenatti has always had a gift

00:04:16.900 --> 00:04:18.630
for scrapping and digging up dirt.

00:04:18.630 --> 00:04:19.590
Before his law career,

00:04:19.590 --> 00:04:21.440
he worked for former Obama Chief of Staff,

00:04:21.440 --> 00:04:23.150
Rahm Emanuel's consulting firm

00:04:23.150 --> 00:04:24.440
where he researched the backgrounds

00:04:24.440 --> 00:04:26.300
of Democratic and Republican candidates

00:04:26.300 --> 00:04:28.040
looking for the details
candidates could use

00:04:28.040 --> 00:04:29.340
to bring down their opponents.

00:04:29.340 --> 00:04:30.320
But after law school,

00:04:30.320 --> 00:04:31.440
Avenatti moved to California

00:04:31.440 --> 00:04:33.360
and became a well-regarded
plaintiff's attorney.

00:04:33.360 --> 00:04:37.440
In 2008, Avenatti won a $32
million judgment against KPMG

00:04:37.440 --> 00:04:38.770
for audit malpractice.

00:04:38.770 --> 00:04:42.550
In 2014, he achieved a $80
million class action settlement

00:04:42.550 --> 00:04:44.230
with the Los Angeles Cemetery,

00:04:44.230 --> 00:04:46.960
which dumped the remains of
Jewish people into a mass grave.

00:04:46.960 --> 00:04:50.670
And in 2017, he secured
a $454 million judgment

00:04:50.670 --> 00:04:53.640
against Kimberly Clark for
defective surgical gowns.

00:04:53.640 --> 00:04:54.523
Although Avenatti claims

00:04:54.523 --> 00:04:57.430
to have won one billion
dollars for his clients,

00:04:57.430 --> 00:05:00.250
many of those verdicts were
substantially reduced on appeal.

00:05:00.250 --> 00:05:01.650
Still by any measure,

00:05:01.650 --> 00:05:04.990
Avenatti had been a very
successful trial lawyer.

00:05:04.990 --> 00:05:07.220
This was enough to get him the
attention of the legal world,

00:05:07.220 --> 00:05:09.360
but outside of it, no
one really knew his name.

00:05:09.360 --> 00:05:12.620
In 2015, he had fewer than
600 Twitter followers,

00:05:12.620 --> 00:05:14.820
most of whom were lawyers and law firms.

00:05:14.820 --> 00:05:16.590
And despite his professional success,

00:05:16.590 --> 00:05:18.900
Avenatti has never been able
to keep his nose for conflict

00:05:18.900 --> 00:05:21.140
from interfering with his
business and professional lives.

00:05:21.140 --> 00:05:22.760
He fought with a former law partner

00:05:22.760 --> 00:05:24.100
resulting in an order requiring him

00:05:24.100 --> 00:05:25.477
to pay one non-equity partner

00:05:25.477 --> 00:05:27.500
$15 million in back pay.

00:05:27.500 --> 00:05:30.260
He then was ordered to pay $4.85 million

00:05:30.260 --> 00:05:32.720
for failing to pay a debt
to a former colleague.

00:05:32.720 --> 00:05:33.850
And eventually his law firm

00:05:33.850 --> 00:05:35.920
was evicted from its Orange County offices

00:05:35.920 --> 00:05:38.280
for allegedly failing
to pay months of rent.

00:05:38.280 --> 00:05:39.350
And Avenatti's troubles

00:05:39.350 --> 00:05:41.110
extended to his other
business connections.

00:05:41.110 --> 00:05:43.780
In 2013, Avenatti purchased
a Seattle coffee chain

00:05:43.780 --> 00:05:44.613
called Tully's.

00:05:44.613 --> 00:05:46.870
His partner in the endeavor
was McDreamy himself,

00:05:46.870 --> 00:05:48.340
no, not Dr. Mike,

00:05:48.340 --> 00:05:50.750
original recipe McDreamy Patrick Dempsey.

00:05:50.750 --> 00:05:51.840
Dempsey actually sued

00:05:51.840 --> 00:05:53.950
to back out of the sale just months later

00:05:53.950 --> 00:05:55.730
alleging that Avenatti had taken on

00:05:55.730 --> 00:05:58.090
a two million dollar
loan without telling him.

00:05:58.090 --> 00:05:59.210
Avenatti purchased the coffee company

00:05:59.210 --> 00:06:01.640
through a larger entity
called Global Baristas.

00:06:01.640 --> 00:06:04.000
Global Baristas struggled to
pay suppliers and landlords

00:06:04.000 --> 00:06:06.290
and had more than 45
lawsuits filed against it.

00:06:06.290 --> 00:06:08.010
The company lost a lawsuit with Curry,

00:06:08.010 --> 00:06:09.300
then a battle with the IRS

00:06:09.300 --> 00:06:12.020
and then closed down all
of the Tully's stores.

00:06:12.020 --> 00:06:13.580
While his businesses were beset

00:06:13.580 --> 00:06:15.400
by litigation and tax problems,

00:06:15.400 --> 00:06:17.800
Avenatti was maintaining
a jetset lifestyle.

00:06:17.800 --> 00:06:19.590
He owned a $19 million mansion,

00:06:19.590 --> 00:06:22.210
numerous expensive race
cars and two private jets.

00:06:22.210 --> 00:06:24.400
But then the bad times came.

00:06:24.400 --> 00:06:25.870
They say bad things happen in threes

00:06:25.870 --> 00:06:27.700
and that's certainly been
true for Michael Avenatti.

00:06:27.700 --> 00:06:29.930
So let's look at the
three most recent cases.

00:06:29.930 --> 00:06:31.730
First is the Nike extortion case.

00:06:31.730 --> 00:06:33.920
Avenatti was arrested in March of 2019

00:06:33.920 --> 00:06:34.870
minutes after tweeting

00:06:34.870 --> 00:06:37.007
that he had scheduled
a press conference to,

00:06:37.007 --> 00:06:41.020
"Disclose a major high
school/basketball scandal

00:06:41.020 --> 00:06:43.790
perpetrated by Nike
that we have uncovered."

00:06:43.790 --> 00:06:45.880
Avenatti was trying to
get ahead of the fallout

00:06:45.880 --> 00:06:47.610
from federal charges they accused Avenatti

00:06:47.610 --> 00:06:49.300
of trying to extort Nike.

00:06:49.300 --> 00:06:50.650
The charges stem from a meeting

00:06:50.650 --> 00:06:51.940
set up by Avenatti with Nike

00:06:51.940 --> 00:06:54.010
that took place on March 19th, 2019.

00:06:54.010 --> 00:06:55.380
The meeting was held under the pretense

00:06:55.380 --> 00:06:57.620
that Avenatti represented
a youth basketball coach

00:06:57.620 --> 00:06:59.280
who had information that Nike employees

00:06:59.280 --> 00:07:01.240
paid the families of high school athletes.

00:07:01.240 --> 00:07:02.740
Avenatti's client was Gary Franklin,

00:07:02.740 --> 00:07:04.040
the coach of a youth basketball team

00:07:04.040 --> 00:07:05.240
called California Supreme.

00:07:05.240 --> 00:07:07.030
The team competed in the AAU leagues,

00:07:07.030 --> 00:07:09.610
which are a hotbed of college
basketball recruiting.

00:07:09.610 --> 00:07:12.330
Major sneaker companies like
Reebok and Nike and Adidas

00:07:12.330 --> 00:07:14.270
all hang around with the
players and college scouts

00:07:14.270 --> 00:07:16.540
creating an atmosphere
of general shadiness.

00:07:16.540 --> 00:07:18.040
And Coach Franklin testified

00:07:18.040 --> 00:07:20.010
that he was bullied by Nike executives

00:07:20.010 --> 00:07:21.970
who forced him to make illicit payments

00:07:21.970 --> 00:07:24.530
to top high school basketball
players and their families.

00:07:24.530 --> 00:07:25.870
Franklin said that in 2018,

00:07:25.870 --> 00:07:27.830
the company ended its
sponsorship of the team

00:07:27.830 --> 00:07:29.560
and he turned to Avenatti for help.

00:07:29.560 --> 00:07:30.393
During this meeting,

00:07:30.393 --> 00:07:33.489
Avenatti demanded that Nike
pay Franklin $1.5 million.

00:07:33.489 --> 00:07:34.910
Avenatti also wanted Nike

00:07:34.910 --> 00:07:36.410
to pay him and his co-counsel,

00:07:36.410 --> 00:07:40.370
famed defense lawyer, Mark
Geragos, $15 to $25 million

00:07:40.370 --> 00:07:44.130
to conduct a internal
investigation on Nike's behalf.

00:07:44.130 --> 00:07:45.580
Avenatti, who allegedly had a debt

00:07:45.580 --> 00:07:47.280
of over $11 million at the time,

00:07:47.280 --> 00:07:48.750
said that he planned an announcement

00:07:48.750 --> 00:07:51.020
on the eve of Nike's
quarterly earnings call

00:07:51.020 --> 00:07:53.130
and at the start of the
annual NCAA tournament

00:07:53.130 --> 00:07:55.980
if they did not agree to
the terms of this agreement.

00:07:55.980 --> 00:07:58.430
Avenatti was betting that
Nike would rather pay him off

00:07:58.430 --> 00:08:00.010
rather than suffer the PR hit,

00:08:00.010 --> 00:08:02.130
which Avenatti predicted
would tank Nike's stock

00:08:02.130 --> 00:08:03.680
by billions of dollars.

00:08:03.680 --> 00:08:05.747
As Avenatti put it in their meeting,

00:08:05.747 --> 00:08:06.980
"The company will die,

00:08:06.980 --> 00:08:08.830
not die, but they are going to incur

00:08:08.830 --> 00:08:10.400
cut after cut after cut.

00:08:10.400 --> 00:08:11.770
And that's what's going to happen

00:08:11.770 --> 00:08:13.800
as soon as this thing becomes public."

00:08:13.800 --> 00:08:15.120
Unfortunately for Avenatti,

00:08:15.120 --> 00:08:18.070
Nike's lawyers who are
some of the best paid

00:08:18.070 --> 00:08:20.710
and most well-regarded
attorneys in the country,

00:08:20.710 --> 00:08:22.550
were not cowed and instead

00:08:22.550 --> 00:08:23.787
called the U.S. attorney's office.

00:08:23.787 --> 00:08:26.950
Nike's lawyers asked
Avenatti to repeat his offer.

00:08:26.950 --> 00:08:29.580
Was he really saying he should
get at least $15 million

00:08:29.580 --> 00:08:31.925
when his client was only
getting $1.5 million?

00:08:31.925 --> 00:08:34.302
Stupidly, not sensing a trap,

00:08:34.302 --> 00:08:36.470
Avenatti said he sure was.

00:08:36.470 --> 00:08:38.170
- [Michael] But I'm not (bleep) around.

00:08:38.170 --> 00:08:40.960
- So why was this an
incredibly foolish move?

00:08:40.960 --> 00:08:42.720
Because the law of extortion.

00:08:42.720 --> 00:08:44.710
The Department of Justice has
traditionally been cautious

00:08:44.710 --> 00:08:46.350
about charging attorneys with extortion.

00:08:46.350 --> 00:08:47.770
That's because it's perfectly legal

00:08:47.770 --> 00:08:49.620
to negotiate a settlement for a client.

00:08:49.620 --> 00:08:51.000
Most civil cases settle.

00:08:51.000 --> 00:08:52.380
Lawyers usually kick things off

00:08:52.380 --> 00:08:54.170
with a demand letter
followed by negotiations

00:08:54.170 --> 00:08:55.580
and sometimes a mediation.

00:08:55.580 --> 00:08:58.820
It's legal to make demands in
the context of negotiations,

00:08:58.820 --> 00:09:00.670
even crazy demands for lots of money.

00:09:00.670 --> 00:09:03.280
So when do a lawyer's
demands become extortion?

00:09:03.280 --> 00:09:05.930
Well, federal extortion is
covered by the Hobbs Act.

00:09:05.930 --> 00:09:06.763
And this is a law

00:09:06.763 --> 00:09:08.960
that makes extortion
and blackmail illegal.

00:09:08.960 --> 00:09:10.947
And the Hobbs Act defines extortion as,

00:09:10.947 --> 00:09:12.470
"The obtaining of property from another,

00:09:12.470 --> 00:09:13.400
with his consent,

00:09:13.400 --> 00:09:16.170
induced by wrongful use of
actual or threatened force,

00:09:16.170 --> 00:09:18.930
violence or fear under
color of official right."

00:09:18.930 --> 00:09:20.400
Now the line between extortion

00:09:20.400 --> 00:09:23.260
and legal settlement negotiations
is not well demarcated.

00:09:23.260 --> 00:09:25.690
A threat of litigation is
generally not considered criminal.

00:09:25.690 --> 00:09:27.960
This is true even if the
lawyer's threats to sue

00:09:27.960 --> 00:09:30.970
are supported by false testimony
or fabricated evidence.

00:09:30.970 --> 00:09:32.570
Federal courts have held that if a lawsuit

00:09:32.570 --> 00:09:34.710
is not pursued exclusively
by lawful methods,

00:09:34.710 --> 00:09:37.180
the threat of litigation
might constitute extortion.

00:09:37.180 --> 00:09:38.520
And the courts look at many factors,

00:09:38.520 --> 00:09:40.890
including whether an entity
like Nike was more afraid

00:09:40.890 --> 00:09:43.320
that its actual illegal
conduct would be exposed

00:09:43.320 --> 00:09:46.450
or more afraid of publicity
from illegitimate threats.

00:09:46.450 --> 00:09:47.830
And if that sounds murky to you,

00:09:47.830 --> 00:09:49.330
that's because it really is.

00:09:49.330 --> 00:09:51.210
Lawyers have a professional responsibility

00:09:51.210 --> 00:09:52.801
to zealously represent their clients

00:09:52.801 --> 00:09:55.580
and Avenatti argued that
he was doing just that

00:09:55.580 --> 00:09:57.660
against a company that
had committed wrongdoing.

00:09:57.660 --> 00:09:59.290
However, if a lawyer starts to stray

00:09:59.290 --> 00:10:01.330
from their responsibility
to their clients,

00:10:01.330 --> 00:10:02.860
that's a huge red flag.

00:10:02.860 --> 00:10:05.100
And this is where
Avenatti ran into trouble.

00:10:05.100 --> 00:10:07.060
When a lawyer uses methods like publicity,

00:10:07.060 --> 00:10:09.880
grossly exaggerated demands
and false information,

00:10:09.880 --> 00:10:12.470
a negotiation can turn
into an illegal shakedown.

00:10:12.470 --> 00:10:15.070
And remember, Avenatti wasn't asking Nike

00:10:15.070 --> 00:10:17.020
to pay the fees Franklin incurred.

00:10:17.020 --> 00:10:20.160
He wanted Nike to pay him and
Geragos millions of dollars

00:10:20.160 --> 00:10:22.560
to act as internal investigators.

00:10:22.560 --> 00:10:23.393
Federal prosecutors

00:10:23.393 --> 00:10:24.960
said Avenatti took advantage of Franklin,

00:10:24.960 --> 00:10:26.100
hoping to use his client

00:10:26.100 --> 00:10:28.090
to pay off his massive personal debt.

00:10:28.090 --> 00:10:29.870
Coach Franklin said he
wanted his team back

00:10:29.870 --> 00:10:31.430
and two Nike executives fired,

00:10:31.430 --> 00:10:34.010
but Avenatti ignored what
his client actually wanted.

00:10:34.010 --> 00:10:37.580
Instead Avenatti started asking
Nike to pay him personally.

00:10:37.580 --> 00:10:38.413
And on top of that,

00:10:38.413 --> 00:10:40.280
he apparently didn't disclose
these payment demands

00:10:40.280 --> 00:10:41.113
to Franklin.

00:10:41.113 --> 00:10:42.460
The coach also said he knew nothing

00:10:42.460 --> 00:10:44.480
about Avenatti's plan to
hold a press conference.

00:10:44.480 --> 00:10:46.330
And this is of course strikingly similar

00:10:46.330 --> 00:10:49.080
to how Avenatti allegedly
handled litigation with Daniels,

00:10:49.080 --> 00:10:51.860
ignoring her stated goals
while pursuing his own.

00:10:51.860 --> 00:10:54.500
The jury obviously was
swayed by the FBI recordings

00:10:54.500 --> 00:10:57.040
where Avenatti pressured
Nike to agree to his terms

00:10:57.040 --> 00:10:58.345
or suffer the consequences.

00:10:58.345 --> 00:11:01.151
- [Michael] And if you don't
want to do that, we're done.

00:11:01.151 --> 00:11:02.273
I'm not (bleep) around with this

00:11:02.273 --> 00:11:05.460
and I'm not continuing to play games.

00:11:05.460 --> 00:11:08.987
You guys know enough now to
know you got a serious problem

00:11:08.987 --> 00:11:11.850
and it's worth more in exposure to me

00:11:11.850 --> 00:11:13.930
to just blow the lid on this thing.

00:11:13.930 --> 00:11:17.270
A few million dollars doesn't
move the needle for me.

00:11:17.270 --> 00:11:19.250
I'm just being really frank with you.

00:11:19.250 --> 00:11:20.810
- Obviously the emphasis
of the phone calls

00:11:20.810 --> 00:11:22.829
appeared to be what was good
for Avenatti personally,

00:11:22.829 --> 00:11:24.637
rather than what was right for his client.

00:11:24.637 --> 00:11:27.160
And the jury probably also
considered Avenatti's threat

00:11:27.160 --> 00:11:29.930
to take $10 billion off
of Nike's market cap

00:11:29.930 --> 00:11:31.820
by disclosing Franklin's allegations.

00:11:31.820 --> 00:11:34.990
This was construed as a grossly
exaggerated economic threat.

00:11:34.990 --> 00:11:36.720
And at the conclusion
of the three week trial,

00:11:36.720 --> 00:11:39.330
a verdict by a unanimous
jury found Michael Avenatti

00:11:39.330 --> 00:11:40.800
guilty on all counts.

00:11:40.800 --> 00:11:43.408
The jury didn't buy the
defense's closing argument that"

00:11:43.408 --> 00:11:45.240
"In the words of Nike itself,

00:11:45.240 --> 00:11:47.970
he went in there to 'Just
Do It' for his client."

00:11:47.970 --> 00:11:49.060
At Avenatti's sentencing,

00:11:49.060 --> 00:11:50.930
he appeared tearful and apologetic.

00:11:50.930 --> 00:11:51.763
He said,

00:11:51.763 --> 00:11:53.640
"I and I alone have destroyed my career,

00:11:53.640 --> 00:11:55.350
my relationships and my life."

00:11:55.350 --> 00:11:56.617
And he continued,

00:11:56.617 --> 00:11:57.450
"Your Honor,

00:11:57.450 --> 00:11:59.330
I've learned that all the fame, notoriety

00:11:59.330 --> 00:12:01.000
and money in the world is meaningless.

00:12:01.000 --> 00:12:03.300
TV and Twitter, Your Honor, mean nothing."

00:12:03.300 --> 00:12:04.950
Amen to that.

00:12:04.950 --> 00:12:07.420
Still, this tactic was
probably a good one.

00:12:07.420 --> 00:12:10.320
He seemed to be getting
reasonably good legal advice

00:12:10.320 --> 00:12:12.390
because the sentence
that he ultimately got

00:12:12.390 --> 00:12:13.520
was significantly lower

00:12:13.520 --> 00:12:15.440
than what the prosecutors
wanted him to get.

00:12:15.440 --> 00:12:18.320
The judge sentenced him to
two and a half years in prison

00:12:18.320 --> 00:12:19.610
and the judge cited two things

00:12:19.610 --> 00:12:21.690
for the downward departure
from the guidelines,

00:12:21.690 --> 00:12:23.710
the horrible conditions
in the Manhattan jail

00:12:23.710 --> 00:12:25.950
where Avenatti spent more than 100 days

00:12:25.950 --> 00:12:27.740
and the absence of Mark Geragos.

00:12:27.740 --> 00:12:29.640
Prosecutors declined to charge Geragos,

00:12:29.640 --> 00:12:31.590
even though he attended all
the meetings with Avenatti

00:12:31.590 --> 00:12:33.960
and played good cop to Avenatti's bad cop.

00:12:33.960 --> 00:12:35.267
The judge called Geragos,

00:12:35.267 --> 00:12:37.710
"A central figure in
the criminal conduct."

00:12:37.710 --> 00:12:39.370
The judge listened to the Avenatti tapes

00:12:39.370 --> 00:12:41.050
and concluded that Geragos' conduct

00:12:41.050 --> 00:12:43.580
simply wasn't distinguishable
from Avenatti.

00:12:43.580 --> 00:12:46.560
And that is an interesting question.

00:12:46.560 --> 00:12:49.520
But that is just the tip
of the Avenatti iceberg

00:12:49.520 --> 00:12:52.150
because he faces continued
legal jeopardy in California,

00:12:52.150 --> 00:12:54.230
where he used to primarily practice.

00:12:54.230 --> 00:12:55.610
Avenatti is now on trial

00:12:55.610 --> 00:12:58.560
for defrauding clients and
absconding with payments to them,

00:12:58.560 --> 00:13:01.260
specifically a federal
grand jury indicted Avenatti

00:13:01.260 --> 00:13:04.810
on 36 counts of fraud,
perjury, failure to pay taxes,

00:13:04.810 --> 00:13:06.860
embezzlement and other financial crimes.

00:13:06.860 --> 00:13:07.820
The indictment alleges

00:13:07.820 --> 00:13:09.770
that Avenatti stole
several million dollars

00:13:09.770 --> 00:13:10.640
from five clients

00:13:10.640 --> 00:13:12.800
and used a variety of Shell
companies and bank accounts

00:13:12.800 --> 00:13:14.090
to cover up his crimes.

00:13:14.090 --> 00:13:15.030
And the crimes range

00:13:15.030 --> 00:13:17.940
from stealing from a mentally
ill paraplegic on disability

00:13:17.940 --> 00:13:19.340
who won a four million settlement

00:13:19.340 --> 00:13:20.980
in a suit against Los Angeles County

00:13:20.980 --> 00:13:22.890
to a $2.5 million settlement

00:13:22.890 --> 00:13:25.882
that was primarily used for
the purchase of a private jet

00:13:25.882 --> 00:13:27.170
allegedly.

00:13:27.170 --> 00:13:30.260
Now, Avenatti is now
acting as his own lawyer

00:13:30.260 --> 00:13:31.590
in these criminal cases,

00:13:31.590 --> 00:13:32.710
meaning that he is literally

00:13:32.710 --> 00:13:34.650
going to cross-examine former clients

00:13:34.650 --> 00:13:37.220
who now accuse him of stealing from them.

00:13:37.220 --> 00:13:38.760
For example, on the first day of trial,

00:13:38.760 --> 00:13:40.590
Avenatti confronted Jeffrey Johnson,

00:13:40.590 --> 00:13:41.800
who Avenatti represented

00:13:41.800 --> 00:13:43.620
in a lawsuit over injuries he sustained

00:13:43.620 --> 00:13:45.600
while jumping from an elevated floor

00:13:45.600 --> 00:13:47.310
in a Los Angeles jail.

00:13:47.310 --> 00:13:48.950
Johnson tried to kill himself twice

00:13:48.950 --> 00:13:50.220
after bullying and attacks

00:13:50.220 --> 00:13:52.390
by inmates and corrections officers.

00:13:52.390 --> 00:13:54.320
Avenatti negotiated a four
million dollar settlement

00:13:54.320 --> 00:13:55.560
with Los Angeles County,

00:13:55.560 --> 00:13:57.530
but the money was put in a medical trust

00:13:57.530 --> 00:13:58.720
that Avenatti controlled.

00:13:58.720 --> 00:13:59.890
Johnson accused Avenatti

00:13:59.890 --> 00:14:00.880
of draining nearly all

00:14:00.880 --> 00:14:02.380
of the four million dollars in settlements

00:14:02.380 --> 00:14:05.170
while paying Johnson
roughly $1,900 a month

00:14:05.170 --> 00:14:07.650
to lull him into thinking
his money was safe.

00:14:07.650 --> 00:14:10.400
Though perversely, Avenatti's
cross-examination of Johnson

00:14:10.400 --> 00:14:11.390
gives us a good window

00:14:11.390 --> 00:14:13.610
on what made him a good trial
lawyer in the first place.

00:14:13.610 --> 00:14:15.050
Avenatti got Johnson to admit

00:14:15.050 --> 00:14:16.910
that he hired Avenatti
to get him out of jail

00:14:16.910 --> 00:14:18.140
by any means necessary

00:14:18.140 --> 00:14:19.540
and that Avenatti delivered.

00:14:19.540 --> 00:14:20.373
Johnson admitted

00:14:20.373 --> 00:14:22.550
that Avenatti got him
released to a medical center

00:14:22.550 --> 00:14:24.570
and that Avenatti paid
the bill for all of it.

00:14:24.570 --> 00:14:26.170
Johnson admits that the care center

00:14:26.170 --> 00:14:27.990
costs several thousand dollars a month

00:14:27.990 --> 00:14:29.700
and that Avenatti paid for that as well.

00:14:29.700 --> 00:14:30.960
And all of this sounds like Avenatti

00:14:30.960 --> 00:14:33.360
did real work on the
case and he probably did,

00:14:33.360 --> 00:14:35.830
but as the prosecution
established on direct examination,

00:14:35.830 --> 00:14:38.270
Avenatti asked Johnson to
sign a peculiar document

00:14:38.270 --> 00:14:40.860
attesting to his ethics
and great work as a lawyer.

00:14:40.860 --> 00:14:42.487
The document says Avenatti is,

00:14:42.487 --> 00:14:44.660
"an exceptional, honest
and ethical attorney

00:14:44.660 --> 00:14:47.470
and I feel fortunate to
have had him represent me."

00:14:47.470 --> 00:14:50.390
There's just one giant
problem with this attestation.

00:14:50.390 --> 00:14:52.820
Avenatti had been charged with
stealing Johnson's settlement

00:14:52.820 --> 00:14:55.130
the day before he asked
Johnson to sign the document

00:14:55.130 --> 00:14:56.870
and Johnson apparently didn't know

00:14:56.870 --> 00:14:59.800
that Avenatti had been accused
of embezzling the funds.

00:14:59.800 --> 00:15:01.200
At any rate, if Avenatti's convicted

00:15:01.200 --> 00:15:03.910
of all of the charges in
the California indictment,

00:15:03.910 --> 00:15:06.309
Avenatti could face many
more years in prison

00:15:06.309 --> 00:15:08.130
on top of the two and a half years,

00:15:08.130 --> 00:15:10.480
he already got from the
New York prosecutors,

00:15:10.480 --> 00:15:12.640
which brings us to the
Stormy Daniels case.

00:15:12.640 --> 00:15:14.660
Later this year, Avenatti will stand trial

00:15:14.660 --> 00:15:16.450
yet a third time

00:15:16.450 --> 00:15:18.910
bringing his time in the
public eye full circle.

00:15:18.910 --> 00:15:20.500
Avenatti will have to defend himself

00:15:20.500 --> 00:15:21.970
against charges of wire fraud

00:15:21.970 --> 00:15:23.440
and aggravated identity theft

00:15:23.440 --> 00:15:25.570
in the case involving Stormy Daniels,

00:15:25.570 --> 00:15:26.870
though, ironically, by this time,

00:15:26.870 --> 00:15:28.530
he'll have quite a lot of experience

00:15:28.530 --> 00:15:29.736
as a criminal defense attorney,

00:15:29.736 --> 00:15:31.990
meaning he might actually
know what he's doing

00:15:31.990 --> 00:15:32.880
this time around.

00:15:32.880 --> 00:15:33.970
Avenatti is accused

00:15:33.970 --> 00:15:36.360
of using misrepresentations
and a fraudulent document

00:15:36.360 --> 00:15:38.960
purporting to bear the
name of Stormy Daniels

00:15:38.960 --> 00:15:40.120
and her own signature

00:15:40.120 --> 00:15:42.070
to convince her literary agent

00:15:42.070 --> 00:15:45.160
to send him the money
the agent owed Daniel's

00:15:45.160 --> 00:15:46.430
for a book advance.

00:15:46.430 --> 00:15:48.500
Once Avenatti fraudulently
obtained the money,

00:15:48.500 --> 00:15:49.460
he allegedly used it

00:15:49.460 --> 00:15:51.440
to pay for typical attorney expenses,

00:15:51.440 --> 00:15:53.900
such as restaurants,
meal delivery services

00:15:53.900 --> 00:15:56.170
and car payments on his Ferrari.

00:15:56.170 --> 00:15:58.480
When Daniel's began to
question where her money was,

00:15:58.480 --> 00:15:59.313
Avenatti lied

00:15:59.313 --> 00:16:01.170
that he was still trying to
obtain it from the publisher

00:16:01.170 --> 00:16:02.760
and then when he finally paid her,

00:16:02.760 --> 00:16:05.670
he did so by improperly diverting
funds from another source.

00:16:05.670 --> 00:16:08.100
This is the same kind of alleged conduct

00:16:08.100 --> 00:16:10.290
that Avenatti pulled with Patrick Dempsey

00:16:10.290 --> 00:16:11.630
and the California clients.

00:16:11.630 --> 00:16:12.880
If Avenatti isn't disbarred

00:16:12.880 --> 00:16:14.780
as a result of the prior two cases,

00:16:14.780 --> 00:16:16.870
if he's found guilty in
this particular case,

00:16:16.870 --> 00:16:18.790
he absolutely will be disbarred

00:16:18.790 --> 00:16:20.260
from ever practicing law again,

00:16:20.260 --> 00:16:22.514
and Avenatti potentially
faces a maximum of 20 years

00:16:22.514 --> 00:16:24.060
for the wire fraud case

00:16:24.060 --> 00:16:26.170
in addition to a mandatory
two year prison sentence

00:16:26.170 --> 00:16:28.370
for the aggravated identity theft charge.

00:16:28.370 --> 00:16:30.080
Oh, and of course, it
won't surprise you to learn

00:16:30.080 --> 00:16:32.600
that Avenatti wasn't shooting
straight with the IRS either.

00:16:32.600 --> 00:16:34.660
During the commission of
all of these alleged crimes,

00:16:34.660 --> 00:16:36.210
Avenatti had a four year streak

00:16:36.210 --> 00:16:38.920
where he did not file
personal income tax returns.

00:16:38.920 --> 00:16:40.200
And three of those years,

00:16:40.200 --> 00:16:43.240
he did not file tax returns
for both of his law firms.

00:16:43.240 --> 00:16:45.270
In addition, he allegedly lied to the IRS

00:16:45.270 --> 00:16:48.350
about pocketing nearly $2.4
million in taxes withheld

00:16:48.350 --> 00:16:51.900
from the paychecks of employees
of Global Baristas U.S.,

00:16:51.900 --> 00:16:54.592
which operated the now
defunct Tully's Coffee stores.

00:16:54.592 --> 00:16:55.910
And in the California case,

00:16:55.910 --> 00:16:57.210
the jury has already heard testimony

00:16:57.210 --> 00:16:59.100
from three other former clients.

00:16:59.100 --> 00:17:01.520
Avenatti allegedly stiffed
YouTuber Michelle Phan,

00:17:01.520 --> 00:17:02.410
a makeup artist,

00:17:02.410 --> 00:17:04.480
out of at least four million dollars.

00:17:04.480 --> 00:17:06.180
Avenatti's former paralegal testified

00:17:06.180 --> 00:17:08.070
that she was instructed to
give Phan the run-around

00:17:08.070 --> 00:17:10.540
on an eight million dollar
wire transfer of funds

00:17:10.540 --> 00:17:12.250
that belonged to Phan.

00:17:12.250 --> 00:17:13.880
Eventually, Avenatti told the paralegal

00:17:13.880 --> 00:17:17.100
to send a partial payment
of four million dollars.

00:17:17.100 --> 00:17:18.510
But Avenatti allegedly embezzled

00:17:18.510 --> 00:17:20.100
the other four million dollars.

00:17:20.100 --> 00:17:22.390
Additionally, Gregory
Barela alleges that Avenatti

00:17:22.390 --> 00:17:24.310
ran his law firm like a Ponzi scheme,

00:17:24.310 --> 00:17:26.310
accusing him of stealing settlement funds

00:17:26.310 --> 00:17:27.740
and then trying to lend him the funds

00:17:27.740 --> 00:17:29.750
at a 10% interest rate.

00:17:29.750 --> 00:17:31.540
Avenatti handled litigation for Barela,

00:17:31.540 --> 00:17:33.830
securing him a settlement
of over two million dollars,

00:17:33.830 --> 00:17:35.210
but in January 2018,

00:17:35.210 --> 00:17:36.840
a third party started paying funds

00:17:36.840 --> 00:17:39.560
into a client trust
fund managed by Avenatti

00:17:39.560 --> 00:17:41.650
for him to pass on to Barela.

00:17:41.650 --> 00:17:43.270
But instead of doing so,

00:17:43.270 --> 00:17:44.800
Avenatti allegedly told Barela

00:17:44.800 --> 00:17:47.100
that the third party had never
paid the settlement money.

00:17:47.100 --> 00:17:50.210
When Barilla hired new lawyers
to get the settlement funds,

00:17:50.210 --> 00:17:51.300
the third party told him

00:17:51.300 --> 00:17:53.430
that they had already
paid money to Avenatti.

00:17:53.430 --> 00:17:55.610
Alexis Gardner hired
Avenatti to represent her

00:17:55.610 --> 00:17:57.100
in a lawsuit against her ex-boyfriend,

00:17:57.100 --> 00:17:59.600
NBA Basketball player Hassan Whiteside.

00:17:59.600 --> 00:18:01.170
Avenatti negotiated a settlement

00:18:01.170 --> 00:18:03.760
of around three million
dollars in early 2017.

00:18:03.760 --> 00:18:04.593
He told Gardner

00:18:04.593 --> 00:18:06.810
that the money would be doled
out in monthly increments,

00:18:06.810 --> 00:18:09.881
but during 2018, the
monthly payments stopped.

00:18:09.881 --> 00:18:11.020
Avenatti assured Gardner

00:18:11.020 --> 00:18:12.940
that he was hounding Whiteside every week

00:18:12.940 --> 00:18:14.240
about missing payments.

00:18:14.240 --> 00:18:15.940
However, prosecutors say that in reality,

00:18:15.940 --> 00:18:17.020
Whiteside's financial team

00:18:17.020 --> 00:18:19.050
had already paid Avenatti a lump sum

00:18:19.050 --> 00:18:21.390
of $2.7 million a year earlier.

00:18:21.390 --> 00:18:22.950
They alleged that Avenatti spent the money

00:18:22.950 --> 00:18:25.290
on private airplanes and other luxuries.

00:18:25.290 --> 00:18:26.660
If these allegations are true,

00:18:26.660 --> 00:18:27.900
Avenatti has committed fraud

00:18:27.900 --> 00:18:29.400
and broken many of the rules

00:18:29.400 --> 00:18:31.760
of professional conduct for lawyers.

00:18:31.760 --> 00:18:33.750
When a lawyer receives
money meant for a client,

00:18:33.750 --> 00:18:35.740
the lawyer doesn't own that money.

00:18:35.740 --> 00:18:37.250
That's common sense.

00:18:37.250 --> 00:18:39.110
The lawyer has to keep that in trust.

00:18:39.110 --> 00:18:42.357
ABA Model Rule 1.5 covers
this exact scenario,

00:18:42.357 --> 00:18:45.250
"A lawyer shall hold property
of clients or third-persons

00:18:45.250 --> 00:18:46.660
that is in the lawyer's possession,

00:18:46.660 --> 00:18:48.130
in connection with their representation,

00:18:48.130 --> 00:18:50.680
separate from the lawyer's own property.

00:18:50.680 --> 00:18:52.340
Funds shall be kept in a separate account,

00:18:52.340 --> 00:18:53.440
maintained in the state

00:18:53.440 --> 00:18:55.110
where the lawyer's office is situated

00:18:55.110 --> 00:18:57.240
or elsewhere with the
consent of the client

00:18:57.240 --> 00:18:58.510
or third-person."

00:18:58.510 --> 00:18:59.880
Although the rules of professional conduct

00:18:59.880 --> 00:19:01.100
are not criminal laws,

00:19:01.100 --> 00:19:03.330
when lawyers misuse client funds,

00:19:03.330 --> 00:19:04.700
